  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough the Weekend...\n\nAfter the upper level low from the weekend moves into MB/ON later\ntoday we will be transitioning into a new pattern for most of the\nwork week. A blocking pattern over the Northern Pacific will \nresult in a strong upper level low situated just off the coast of \nthe PNW which will keep SW flow over the western parts of the CWA \nfor much of the week. There will then be an area of relative high \npressure over much of the central and eastern CONUS keeping our \neastern zones mostly under the influence of that ridge. We will be\nin the \"battle zone\" between these features until the block\npotentially breaks down late week.\n\nWith this southwesterly flow, we will see several shortwaves move\nthrough the area bringing repeated chances for showers and \nstorms. The first of which should arrive Tuesday afternoon bringing\na chance for isolated to scattered showers and storms across the \nregion, mostly in the mountains. Better chances of showers come \nWednesday with a slightly stronger vort max with likely PoPs \nforecast in the mountains. None of the showers and storms will be\nefficient rainfall producers due to the lack of moisture. PWATs \nTuesday do not even touch 1\" on the 90th percentile while the mean\nsits around 0.5\" of the NBM with Wednesday values only a tenth or\ntwo higher. In terms of severe, the only concern I see as of now \nwould be strong wind gusts associated with downburst activity / \ncollapsing showers and storms. \n\nTemperatures will return to normal today and tomorrow with\nwidespread highs in the upper 70s and low 80s. Following this\nthere may be a notable difference between the highs in our eastern\nplains versus the foothills in the west as the two aforementioned \nregimes jockey for dominance. Places like Baker and Miles City may\nsee highs 10F above average into the 90s while Livingston keeps \ntemps a touch below average in the 70s. Winds will be a little \nbreezy Wednesday and Thursday with the advancing shortwaves but \nmodels have backed off with this morning's runs to what appear to \nbe more reasonable values. Widespread gusts in the the 20s mph are\nforecast both days with slightly higher (30s mph with isolated up\nto 40 mph) in the gap areas of the south-centeral Montana \nfoothills. These conditions combined with Min RH values in the \nteens to low 20s will result in elevated fire weather concerns \nboth Wednesday and Thursday, esepcailly low elevations west of \nBillings. Locations in the higher terrain recently received 0.5-1\"\nof precip and thus fire concerns decrease notably with elevation.\n\nAll weather elements this weekend still have several question \nmarks around it. It looks like the blocking pattern will start to \nbreak down a bit which could result in a progressive pattern \nemerging to kick off meteorological fall. The GFS however, is also\nshowing the remnants of a strong hurricane getting ingested by \nthe southwesterly monsoonal flow which could bring very wet \nconditions to our area. Or there is still a slight chance that the \nblock could maintain its position a little longer and/or the \ntropical system could not develop / miss the track entirely and \nthen we just remain under the ridge. Those with interests in this \nweekend's weather should frequently check back as the forecast \nwill likely chance several more times before the end of the work \nweek. WMR",
